1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,294
In padel, whoever controls the net zone wins
eighty to eighty-three percent of all

2
00:00:05,294 --> 00:00:05,732
points.

3
00:00:06,082 --> 00:00:10,987
That is why opponents constantly lob deep into
your backcourt: to push you off the

4
00:00:10,987 --> 00:00:11,223
net.

5
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:16,535
If you come from tennis, your instinct is to
jump, wind up with full force,

6
00:00:16,535 --> 00:00:19,146
and try to smash the ball into the turf.

7
00:00:19,497 --> 00:00:24,084
Two seconds later, you are watching your
opponents celebrate an easy winner.

8
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:29,711
Your bandeja is not weak because you lack power;
it is weak because you are treating

9
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:30,705
it like a smash.

10
00:00:31,055 --> 00:00:36,346
In professional padel, the bandeja accounts
for up to sixty-nine percent of all

11
00:00:36,346 --> 00:00:37,007
overheads.

12
00:00:37,357 --> 00:00:42,582
It is not an attack designed to end the point;
it is a tactical reset to keep you at

13
00:00:42,582 --> 00:00:43,074
the net.

14
00:00:43,425 --> 00:00:48,681
Today, we break down the physics behind the
five mistakes ruining your bandeja,

15
00:00:48,681 --> 00:00:51,638
and the exact drills to make
it unattackable.

16
00:00:51,988 --> 00:00:56,447
The biggest trap in padel is trying to
crush a deep lob with flat power.

17
00:00:56,797 --> 00:01:00,552
In tennis, a hard downward smash
wins the point outright.

18
00:01:00,901 --> 00:01:07,149
In padel, the court is surrounded by rigid
tempered glass walls with a high rebound

19
00:01:07,149 --> 00:01:11,760
coefficient of zero-point-seven-zero
to zero-point-seven-five.

20
00:01:12,110 --> 00:01:14,934
When you hit a flat overhead
from deep court,

21
00:01:14,934 --> 00:01:18,617
the ball slams into the rear glass
with high kinetic energy.

22
00:01:18,967 --> 00:01:22,420
Because the glass is rigid and the
ball has no backspin,

23
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:26,903
it explodes outward at waist-to-chest
height into the center of the court.

24
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:31,844
You have just fed your opponents a hanging
setup that they crush with an offensive

25
00:01:31,844 --> 00:01:33,504
wall smash right at your feet.

26
00:01:33,853 --> 00:01:39,158
Match analytics show that attempting flat
smashes from deep court leads to a

27
00:01:39,158 --> 00:01:44,050
four-fold increase in unforced errors
compared to a controlled bandeja.

28
00:01:44,401 --> 00:01:49,789
Hitting a flat smash against the glass is like
throwing a rubber ball at a concrete

29
00:01:49,789 --> 00:01:53,830
wall: the harder you throw it, the
faster it flies back at you.

30
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:59,108
The bandeja shifts your mindset from winning
the point to neutralizing the lob.

31
00:01:59,458 --> 00:02:04,830
Your goal is to slice the ball so it hits the
back glass softly and dies along the

32
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:05,218
floor.

33
00:02:05,568 --> 00:02:07,808
Master this with the Glass Ceiling Drill.

34
00:02:08,158 --> 00:02:12,894
Place target tape on the back glass
zero-point-eight meters above the turf.

35
00:02:13,244 --> 00:02:16,804
Have a partner feed deep lobs
while you hit bandejas,

36
00:02:16,804 --> 00:02:20,561
aiming for every ball to strike the
glass below the tape.

37
00:02:20,911 --> 00:02:26,116
Perform four sets of twelve balls, aiming
for seventy-five percent accuracy.

38
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:31,854
The second mistake happens before you even
swing: letting your non-dominant arm hang

39
00:02:31,854 --> 00:02:32,931
down by your hip.

40
00:02:33,281 --> 00:02:38,146
When a lob goes up, amateur players turn
their bodies but leave their left arm

41
00:02:38,146 --> 00:02:38,700
dangling.

42
00:02:39,049 --> 00:02:43,102
Biomechanically, that dropped arm
ruins your entire stroke.

43
00:02:43,453 --> 00:02:47,592
Your non-dominant arm acts as a
critical kinetic counterweight.

44
00:02:47,941 --> 00:02:53,853
Extending your non-hitting arm upward at a
forty-five-degree angle activates the

45
00:02:53,853 --> 00:02:56,773
serratus anterior and trapezius muscles.

46
00:02:57,123 --> 00:02:59,636
This anchors upward scapular rotation,

47
00:02:59,636 --> 00:03:03,566
keeping your hitting shoulder
elevated in the scapular plane.

48
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:06,359
When you drop your non-dominant arm,

49
00:03:06,359 --> 00:03:11,641
your upper torso collapses forward and your
hitting shoulder dips by fifteen to

50
00:03:11,641 --> 00:03:12,961
twenty-five degrees.

51
00:03:13,311 --> 00:03:16,274
Your chest uncoils
prematurely toward the net,

52
00:03:16,274 --> 00:03:21,443
turning your cutting stroke into an off-balance
push that dumps the ball into the

53
00:03:21,443 --> 00:03:21,695
net.

54
00:03:22,045 --> 00:03:27,465
Extending your left arm is like an archer
holding a bow: the bow hand holds the

55
00:03:27,465 --> 00:03:30,920
target steady while the draw
hand pulls the string.

56
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:35,302
Point your left hand directly at the
falling ball to track its flight.

57
00:03:35,652 --> 00:03:38,661
Keep that arm extended until
contact begins,

58
00:03:38,661 --> 00:03:43,140
then tuck it into your chest as you
swing to control your rotation.

59
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:48,958
Practice the Left-Hand Catch Drill: move back on
high lobs with your paddle prepared,

60
00:03:48,958 --> 00:03:53,346
and catch the falling ball with your
extended left hand at eye level.

61
00:03:53,696 --> 00:03:56,133
Complete three sets of ten catches,

62
00:03:56,133 --> 00:04:01,141
ensuring your left arm stays locked at
forty-five degrees until the catch.

63
00:04:01,491 --> 00:04:05,224
Watch where amateur players strike
the ball on a bandeja.

64
00:04:05,575 --> 00:04:10,491
Most let the lob float directly over their
crown, hyperextend their spine,

65
00:04:10,491 --> 00:04:13,703
reach up behind their head, and
swat at the ball.

66
00:04:14,053 --> 00:04:18,874
Striking behind your head destroys both your
control and your shoulder health.

67
00:04:19,224 --> 00:04:24,216
In tennis, you strike an overhead at full
vertical extension at twelve o'clock.

68
00:04:24,566 --> 00:04:30,379
In padel, reaching for full vertical extension
forces a downward attack angle that

69
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:32,971
triggers the high glass rebound trap.

70
00:04:33,321 --> 00:04:38,454
Worse, striking behind your coronal plane
forces the shoulder into extreme

71
00:04:38,454 --> 00:04:43,177
horizontal abduction, pinching the
supraspinatus tendon against bone.

72
00:04:43,527 --> 00:04:49,390
Clinical data shows that rotator cuff tendinitis
accounts for over seven percent of

73
00:04:49,390 --> 00:04:50,716
all padel injuries.

74
00:04:51,067 --> 00:04:54,547
The optimal contact point is at
eye-to-forehead level,

75
00:04:54,547 --> 00:04:58,406
about one-point-eight to
two-point-two meters off the ground.

76
00:04:58,755 --> 00:05:02,206
You want the ball twelve to eighteen
inches in front of you,

77
00:05:02,206 --> 00:05:06,392
positioned at the one o'clock angle to the
side of your dominant shoulder.

78
00:05:06,743 --> 00:05:11,915
Unlike a tennis smash, your hitting elbow
should remain flexed between one hundred

79
00:05:11,915 --> 00:05:14,594
and ten and one hundred and
thirty degrees.

80
00:05:14,943 --> 00:05:20,663
In Spanish, bandeja means serving tray: hold
your paddle flat beside your head like

81
00:05:20,663 --> 00:05:23,114
a waiter balancing a tray of drinks.

82
00:05:23,464 --> 00:05:28,500
Holding the tray at eye level with a bent elbow
gives you complete leverage without

83
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:30,419
straining your shoulder capsule.

84
00:05:30,769 --> 00:05:35,848
If you feel shoulder pain lifting your arm
between seventy and one hundred and

85
00:05:35,848 --> 00:05:40,284
twenty degrees, stop overheads
immediately and see a physiotherapist.

86
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:45,799
To generate spin, amateur players frequently
snap their wrist violently downward

87
00:05:45,799 --> 00:05:46,820
through contact.

88
00:05:47,170 --> 00:05:49,730
This is a massive mechanical mistake.

89
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:54,475
The ball contacts your carbon paddle for
just three to five milliseconds.

90
00:05:54,825 --> 00:06:00,164
Trying to snap your wrist during that tiny
window creates wild trajectory errors,

91
00:06:00,164 --> 00:06:04,852
where a three-degree wrist tilt changes
landing depth by over five feet.

92
00:06:05,201 --> 00:06:10,965
Even worse, repetitive eccentric wrist snapping
is the leading cause of tennis elbow,

93
00:06:10,965 --> 00:06:15,121
which represents over twenty percent
of all injuries in padel.

94
00:06:15,471 --> 00:06:18,562
To protect your tendons and
control the ball,

95
00:06:18,562 --> 00:06:24,138
lock your wrist in a firm Continental grip and
generate slice with your entire arm

96
00:06:24,138 --> 00:06:24,474
unit.

97
00:06:24,824 --> 00:06:27,544
The paddle must follow a high-to-low,

98
00:06:27,544 --> 00:06:33,056
outside-to-inside diagonal cutting path with
the paddle face open fifteen to

99
00:06:33,056 --> 00:06:34,488
twenty-five degrees.

100
00:06:34,838 --> 00:06:40,288
Visualize the ball as a clock face: cut across
the ball starting at two o'clock and

101
00:06:40,288 --> 00:06:42,753
carve underneath toward seven o'clock.

102
00:06:43,103 --> 00:06:46,602
This cutting action generates
horizontal backspin.

103
00:06:46,951 --> 00:06:49,985
When a ball with backspin hits
the vertical glass,

104
00:06:49,985 --> 00:06:53,138
friction pulls the ball downward
along the wall face.

105
00:06:53,488 --> 00:06:56,521
Instead of rebounding outward
at waist height,

106
00:06:56,521 --> 00:07:01,877
the sliced ball slides down the glass and
bounces under zero-point-five meters off

107
00:07:01,877 --> 00:07:03,813
the turf, dying in the corner.

108
00:07:04,163 --> 00:07:09,518
Slicing the bandeja is like carving a thin
slice off a loaf of bread: you do not

109
00:07:09,518 --> 00:07:14,211
chop down with a cleaver; you draw the
blade smoothly across the crust.

110
00:07:14,561 --> 00:07:17,228
Train this with the
Two-to-Seven Clock Drill.

111
00:07:17,578 --> 00:07:21,333
From the service line, have a partner
feed chest-high floaters.

112
00:07:21,683 --> 00:07:25,639
Carve across the bottom-outside of
the ball with a firm wrist,

113
00:07:25,639 --> 00:07:28,339
aiming deep into the
opposite corner glass.

114
00:07:28,689 --> 00:07:34,082
Hit four sets of fifteen balls, aiming for
eleven out of fifteen to rebound within

115
00:07:34,082 --> 00:07:35,836
one meter of the back wall.

116
00:07:36,185 --> 00:07:41,540
The final mistake is not about how you swing; it
is about what you do after you hit.

117
00:07:41,890 --> 00:07:47,154
Most intermediate players hit their bandeja and
freeze in the middle of the court to

118
00:07:47,154 --> 00:07:48,269
admire their shot.

119
00:07:48,619 --> 00:07:52,756
Standing three to six meters from the
net puts you in no-man's-land,

120
00:07:52,756 --> 00:07:54,614
surrendering the net advantage.

121
00:07:54,963 --> 00:08:00,035
Furthermore, backpedaling heel-to-toe shifts
your center of gravity behind your

122
00:08:00,035 --> 00:08:04,030
heels, risking severe falls and acute
Achilles tendon ruptures.

123
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:09,635
The proper footwork is the backward chassé:
pivot your feet ninety degrees sideways

124
00:08:09,635 --> 00:08:14,703
on the first frame of the lob, and glide
backward on the balls of your feet with

125
00:08:14,703 --> 00:08:15,516
knees flexed.

126
00:08:15,866 --> 00:08:20,218
Plant your back foot into the turf to
establish a stable kinetic base.

127
00:08:20,568 --> 00:08:24,807
As you cut through the ball, transfer your
weight onto your front foot,

128
00:08:24,807 --> 00:08:27,928
pushing you naturally forward
into a recovery sprint.

129
00:08:28,278 --> 00:08:33,287
Your stroke is not finished until you sprint
forward and split-step at the net

130
00:08:33,287 --> 00:08:37,281
dominance line, two to two-point-five
meters from the net tape.

131
00:08:37,631 --> 00:08:40,852
Master this with the
Bandeja-to-Volley Shuttle Drill.

132
00:08:41,202 --> 00:08:46,503
Start at the net line, chassé backward behind
the service line to hit a bandeja,

133
00:08:46,503 --> 00:08:52,066
and immediately sprint forward to the two-meter
net dominance line before blocking a

134
00:08:52,066 --> 00:08:52,786
low volley.

135
00:08:53,136 --> 00:08:56,422
Complete five sets of six full
shuttle sequences,

136
00:08:56,422 --> 00:09:01,285
keeping your backward footwork sideways and
your forward sprint explosive.

137
00:09:01,635 --> 00:09:04,841
To turn these five fixes into
permanent instincts,

138
00:09:04,841 --> 00:09:08,739
dedicate your next month of training
to three weekly sessions.

139
00:09:09,089 --> 00:09:14,312
In week one, eliminate the flat smash:
practice the glass ceiling drill until

140
00:09:14,312 --> 00:09:18,732
seventy-five percent of your overheads
stay below the target line.

141
00:09:19,082 --> 00:09:24,633
In week two, lock the tracking arm: practice the
left-hand catch drill to keep your

142
00:09:24,633 --> 00:09:27,871
shoulder plane elevated and
your eye on the ball.

143
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:33,615
In week three, groove the contact point:
pre-set the serving tray at one o'clock

144
00:09:33,615 --> 00:09:37,544
with a flexed elbow and carve with a
firm Continental grip.

145
00:09:37,894 --> 00:09:43,077
In week four, connect the footwork loop:
combine backward chassé steps with an

146
00:09:43,077 --> 00:09:47,473
immediate recovery sprint back to the
two-meter net dominance line.

147
00:09:47,823 --> 00:09:52,303
Master the bandeja, and deep lobs will
no longer push you around.

148
00:09:52,653 --> 00:09:56,528
You will neutralize aggressive
attacks, control the glass,

149
00:09:56,528 --> 00:09:59,352
and dominate the net in
every single match.

150
00:09:59,701 --> 00:10:01,578
See you on the padel courts.
